---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { url } from '../../../lib/site';
const release = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve('public/downloads/extension-release.json'), 'utf8'));
---
<Base title="Before You Give: compatibility" description="Exact extension URLs, privacy boundaries and downloadable package checksums.">
  <header class="page-head"><div class="wrap"><Crumbs items={[{label:'Tools',href:'/tools/'},{label:'Browser Extension',href:'/tools/browser-extension/'},{label:'Compatibility'}]} /><h1>Before You Give: compatibility</h1><p class="lede">Version {release.extensionVersion}. A local checklist and an explicit registry lookup.</p></div></header>
  <article class="wrap section-tight prose">
    <h2>Where the card can appear</h2>
    <p>The manifest declares these four HTTPS URL patterns. They identify URLs, not whether a page is a fundraiser or whether an organization is registered. Browser permissions and restricted-page rules still apply.</p>
    <ul>{release.supportedPatterns.map((pattern: string) => <li><code>{pattern}</code></li>)}</ul>
    <p>Every path on the exact <code>www.justgiving.com</code> and <code>www.every.org</code> hosts matches, including information or account pages. Other subdomains and the bare JustGiving/Every.org hosts are outside this list. HTTP and lookalike domains are outside it too.</p>
    <p>The matrix models Chromium’s path rules, including ordinary percent-encoding and its trailing <code>/*</code> shortcut: the GoFundMe <code>/f</code> prefix can match without a final slash. Queries are part of path matching; fragments are excluded. These modeled cases are not installed Firefox compatibility evidence. Navigation inside a page without a document reload has no reinjection guarantee.</p>
    <h2>The toolbar and your privacy</h2>
    <p>The popup offers ten checklist questions and an editable search. When active-tab access is available, it suggests the title. The card reads the title only after a lookup click. The search uses a browser-only URL fragment; external registries receive a name only when you choose their link.</p>
    <p>Search input is limited to 120 characters. Failed tab opening leaves the text and a direct-link option. If the text changes while opening fails, submit the current text again. A failed hide preference leaves a visible notice and a Close control; it does not claim the preference was saved.</p>
    <p>Permissions remain <code>activeTab</code> and <code>storage</code>. There is no background worker, analytics, donation handling or background page-text transmission. <a href={url('/tools/browser-extension/privacy/')}>Read the privacy notice</a>.</p>
    <h2>What has been checked</h2>
    <p>Automated checks execute the actual popup and card with synthetic DOM/API fixtures: 32 URL cases and 25 privacy/recovery groups. Independent ZIP/CRC checks, hostile-input refusal, source correspondence and reproducible builds cover all three packages. Checksums identify exact bytes; they do not establish an approved store listing.</p>
    <p>Installed Chrome, Edge, Firefox, mobile, incognito and frame behavior, store approval and a comprehensive accessibility audit remain unverified. A normal HTML developer preview is a separate bounded check. The manifest declares Firefox desktop 140 and Android 142 minimums; those declarations are not device test results.</p>
    <p>Matching references: <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/match-patterns">Chrome documentation</a>, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Mozilla/Add-ons/WebExtensions/Match_patterns">Mozilla documentation</a> and <a href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/refs/heads/main/extensions/common/url_pattern.cc">Chromium’s implementation</a>. These were inspected October 27, 2026.</p>
    <h2>Exact packages</h2>
    <p>Source SHA-256: <code>{release.sourceSha256}</code>. The source digest covers the ordered file paths, byte lengths and SHA-256 values in the <a href={url('/downloads/extension-release.json')}>release manifest</a>. The manifest also contains package hashes, declared engines and empty store-listing records.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Bytes</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody>{release.packages.map((pkg: {path:string;bytes:number;sha256:string}) => <tr><td><a href={url(`/downloads/${pkg.path}`)} download>{pkg.path}</a></td><td>{pkg.bytes.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td><code>{pkg.sha256}</code></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p><a href={url('/tools/browser-extension/')}>Developer installation instructions</a>. Store submission and acceptance remain owner-controlled steps; no listing is claimed.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>code {overflow-wrap:anywhere;} td code {white-space:normal;}</style>
